      altTranslation: "She who bore a parrot-voiced woman —\nlike a flower wilting in the north wind, with abundant tears:\nseeing the spotless lotus-eyed one depart in haste —\nKauśalyā who rose and wept with her hair unbound and tangled.",
      theme: "Hair Unbound — Kauśalyā's unbound hair (vēr muṭi — hair still tangled from sleep or grief) is the physical sign of her extreme distress. Grief undoes the careful order of appearance.",
      note: "In Tamil and Sanskrit literary convention, unbound hair (muṭi aḻittal) signifies extreme grief or distress — the breaking of the normal order of self-presentation. Kauśalyā did not have time to bind her hair; she rose as she was. This is the intimacy of sudden, unmediated grief."
